   $q$Which revision of sentence 11 gives the story the strongest sense of closure?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Finally, the tide came back in, and we went to a restaurant for a big dinner.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"starts-new-event"},{"text":"Finally, the tide came back in, so the tide pools were filled with water again.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"detail-not-closure"},{"text":"Finally, the tide came back in, and tides at most beaches happen twice a day.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"adds-fact-not-closure"},{"text":"Finally, the tide came back in, and as we left, I whispered goodbye to the little crab.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Finally, the tide came back in, and as we left, I whispered goodbye to the little crab.$q$, $q$Saying goodbye to the crab ties the ending back to the creature the writer discovered, which closes the story. The restaurant revision starts a new event instead of finishing the tide pool visit.$q$, 'draft', '23ddbd72-bc76-58e8-b44e-a11008caf5a5')
